/* ============================================================
   Q1. What is a CTE, and how does it improve readability?
   ============================================================
   A Common Table Expression (CTE) is a temporary, named result set
   defined with the WITH keyword. It exists only for the single
   query that follows it.

   Syntax:
       WITH cte_name AS (
           SELECT ...
       )
       SELECT ... FROM cte_name;

   How it improves readability:
   1. Breaks a complex query into small, named, logical steps
      (instead of deeply nested subqueries).
   2. The query reads top-to-bottom, like a story.
   3. The same CTE can be referenced several times in one query,
      so the logic is not repeated.
   4. Supports recursion (e.g., employee -> manager hierarchies).
   5. Easier to debug: each CTE can be tested on its own.
   ============================================================ */


/* ============================================================
   Q2. Why are some views updatable while others are read-only?
   ============================================================
   A view is only updatable when every row in the view maps to
   exactly ONE row in ONE underlying table, so the database knows
   which base row to change.

   A view is UPDATABLE when it:
   - selects from a single table
   - has simple column references (no calculations)
   - uses no DISTINCT, GROUP BY, HAVING, aggregate functions
     (SUM, AVG, COUNT...), UNION, or window functions

   A view is READ-ONLY when it uses any of the above, because one
   view row may represent many base rows (or a computed value
   that does not exist in any table).

   Example:
       -- Updatable
       CREATE VIEW v_simple AS
       SELECT ProductID, ProductName, Price FROM Products;

       -- Read-only (uses GROUP BY + AVG)
       CREATE VIEW v_summary AS
       SELECT Category, AVG(Price) AS AvgPrice
       FROM Products GROUP BY Category;

       -- UPDATE v_summary SET AvgPrice = 1000;  -> ERROR
       --   (which product's price should change?)
   ============================================================ */


/* ============================================================
   Q3. Advantages of stored procedures over repeated raw SQL
   ============================================================
   A stored procedure is a named, saved block of SQL stored in
   the database and run with CALL procedure_name(...).

   1. Reusability   - write once, call many times.
   2. Maintainability - change the logic in one place only.
   3. Performance   - compiled/cached on the server, less
      network traffic (one CALL instead of many statements).
   4. Security      - users can be given permission to run the
      procedure without direct access to the tables; helps
      prevent SQL injection when parameters are used.
   5. Consistency   - everyone runs the same business logic.
   6. Supports parameters, variables, loops, conditions and
      transactions (full programming logic).
   ============================================================ */


/* ============================================================
   Q4. Purpose of triggers + one essential use case
   ============================================================
   A trigger is a block of SQL that runs AUTOMATICALLY when an
   INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE happens on a table (BEFORE or AFTER).

   Purposes:
   - Enforce business rules and data integrity
   - Auditing / logging changes
   - Automatically maintain related or derived data
   - Validate or modify data before it is saved

   Essential use case: AUDIT / ARCHIVE
   When a product is deleted, a trigger automatically copies the
   deleted row into an archive table. This works no matter which
   application or user runs the DELETE, so no deleted data is
   ever lost (see Q10).
   ============================================================ */


/* ============================================================
   Q5. Need for data modelling and normalization
   ============================================================
   DATA MODELLING = designing the structure of the database
   (entities/tables, attributes/columns, relationships, keys)
   BEFORE creating it (ER diagram -> tables).

   Why it is needed:
   - Gives a clear blueprint that business and developers agree on
   - Ensures data is stored logically and relationships are correct
   - Reduces rework and design mistakes later

   NORMALIZATION = organizing tables into smaller related tables
   (1NF, 2NF, 3NF...) to remove redundancy.

   Why it is needed:
   1. Removes duplicate data (saves storage)
   2. Prevents anomalies:
        - Insert anomaly (cannot add data without other data)
        - Update anomaly (must change the same data in many rows)
        - Delete anomaly (deleting a row loses unrelated data)
   3. Keeps data consistent and accurate
   4. Makes the database easier to maintain and extend

   Example: Products and Sales are kept in separate tables linked
   by ProductID, instead of repeating product name and price in
   every sale row.
   ============================================================ */



